@prefix : <#> .
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix onto: <../ontology.ttl#> .
@prefix oplcert: <http://www.openlinksw.com/schemas/cert#> .

<> a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:name "WebID vs Canonical Identity — Protocol Credential Distinction"@en ;
    schema:description "Gates the distinction between a person's canonical identity and their certificate-bound WebID/NetID protocol credential. A WebID is an identifier used WITH the WebID-TLS protocol — it represents identity within that protocol but is not the identity itself."@en ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-08-01T00:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:dateModified "2026-08-01T00:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:author <https://linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this> ;
    schema:about :webidVsCanonicalIdentity ;
    schema:step :step-distinguishCanonical,
        :step-presentWebidAsCredential,
        :step-whoamiWebIdLine,
        :step-multipleWebids .

:webidVsCanonicalIdentity a schema:Thing ;
    schema:name "WebID vs Canonical Identity Distinction"@en ;
    schema:description "Protocol credential vs person identity: a WebID/NetID is a certificate-bound identifier used with mTLS/WebID-TLS. A canonical identity names the person independent of any protocol."@en .

# ── Step 1: Distinguish the two concepts ──────────────────────────────────────

:step-distinguishCanonical a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "Always distinguish canonical identity from WebID/NetID protocol credential"@en ;
    schema:text """Two distinct concepts that must never be conflated:

CANONICAL IDENTITY — who a person IS:
  • Independent of any authentication protocol
  • Typically a social platform IRI (LinkedIn, X, etc.) or a DBpedia IRI
  • Example: https://www.linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this

WEBID / NETID — a protocol credential:
  • Certificate-bound — the SAN URI in the client certificate
  • Used WITH the WebID-TLS/mTLS protocol for authentication
  • A person may have multiple WebIDs (different certs, different bundles)
  • Each WebID has owl:sameAs links to the canonical identity
  • Example: https://kingsley.idehen.net/DAV/home/kidehen/Public/YouID/link-in-bio-credentials-5/index.html#netid

RULE: Never say 'your WebID is your identity.' A WebID represents identity within the WebID-TLS protocol — it is not the identity itself."""@en .

# ── Step 2: Present WebID as a credential when asked ─────────────────────────

:step-presentWebidAsCredential a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "When asked 'what is my WebID?', present it as a protocol credential and name the canonical identity separately"@en ;
    schema:text """Response template:
  • Canonical identity: {canonical IRI}
  • WebID (certificate-bound, for mTLS): {SAN URI}

Always include BOTH — the canonical identity tells you WHO the person is; the WebID tells you WHICH certificate-bound credential to use with the protocol."""@en .

# ── Step 3: Whoami WebID line shows cert-bound URI ───────────────────────────

:step-whoamiWebIdLine a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "In whoami output, the WebID line shows the cert-bound SAN URI, not a platform IRI"@en ;
    schema:text "The whoami 'WebID:' line carries the certificate-bound SAN URI (with ✅/⚠ badge). The canonical identity is expressed through the Name line + the owl:sameAs hyperlink line. Never put a LinkedIn or other platform IRI on the WebID line — those go in owl:sameAs."@en .

# ── Step 4: Acknowledge multiple WebIDs ──────────────────────────────────────

:step-multipleWebids a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "4"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "Acknowledge that a person may have multiple WebIDs for different cert bundles"@en ;
    schema:text "A single person (canonical identity) can have any number of WebIDs, each bound to a different certificate. When multiple credential bundles exist (link-in-bio-credentials-5, -22, -23, -24, etc.), each has its own WebID. When asked 'what is my WebID?', present the one from the primary/active bundle and note that others exist."@en .
